\title{Carian Language Resources for K-12 Curriculum Development}
\author{Compiled by Claude Sonnet 5.5 (claude-sonnet-5-5) for Andr\'as Kornai}
\date{}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Carian (ISO 639-3 xcr; Glottocode cari1274) is an extinct Anatolian language of southwestern Anatolia, written in its own alphabet. It was deciphered mainly through Egyptian--Carian bilingual tomb inscriptions, beginning with John Ray in 1981. Adiego and Schürr then developed the Ray--Schürr--Adiego values, which the Kaunos Carian--Greek bilingual of 1996 largely confirmed. Much of the evidence is onomastic, and many letter values are still provisional. I found no school materials.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
I found no primers, textbooks or school programmes for Carian.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Ignasi-Xavier Adiego, \emph{The Carian Language} (Brill, 2007): handbook covering the inscriptions, decipherment history, alphabet, phonology, morphology and vocabulary. Search results showed a table of contents at \url{https://library.arce.org/portal/Record/36777/TOC}
\item Wikipedia, ``Carian alphabets'' (the Carian script article). \url{https://www.Wikipedia.com/wiki/Carian_script}
\item Unicode document N3020 on the encoding of Carian characters. \url{https://unicode.org/wg2/docs/n3020.pdf}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
I found no religious texts or community organisations.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
I found no audio or software. The Unicode proposal is the only technical resource found. Several Adiego papers appeared on academia.edu, but I did not check them and do not cite them.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
The language is extinct, the corpus is small and mostly names, and the decipherment is partial. A K-12 unit could use the script, bilingual inscriptions and the decipherment story, with the sources above as the basis. It could not teach the language itself.
\end{document}
